-- title:
--   Admissibility of a modulus descends to divisors of the degree
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field, $L$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, $m, n$ natural numbers with $m \mid n$ and $n \neq 0$, and $\mathfrak f$ an ideal of $\mathcal O_K$. Assume `IsAdmissibleModulusOfDegree K L n 𝔣`, that is: $\mathfrak f \neq 0$ and, for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_K$ whose inertia subgroup in $L \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[K]} L$ at the chosen prime `primeAbove K L v` of $L$ above $v$ is nontrivial, one has $v^{d_n(v)} \mid \mathfrak f$, where $$d_n(v) = \mathtt{admissibleExpOfDegree}\,K\,n\,v = 1 + \sum_{p \mid n} \bigl(v_p(n) + 1\bigr)\, e\bigl(p\mathbb Z \mid v\bigr),$$ the sum running over the prime factors $p$ of $n$, with $v_p(n)$ the exponent of $p$ in the factorisation of $n$ and $e(p\mathbb Z \mid v)$ the ramification index `Ideal.ramificationIdx'` of the ideal generated by $p$ in $\mathbb Z$ with respect to $v$. The conclusion is `IsAdmissibleModulusOfDegree K L m 𝔣`: the same nonvanishing of $\mathfrak f$, and $v^{d_m(v)} \mid \mathfrak f$ at every such prime $v$.
--
--   The predicate `IsAdmissibleModulusOfDegree` prescribes, for a given degree, a lower bound on the local depth of a modulus $\mathfrak f$ at the primes of $K$ with nontrivial inertia in $L$; this lemma records that the prescription weakens as the degree is replaced by a divisor. It is used in the first-inequality bookkeeping for idele class groups, in [`NumberField.NormIndex.ideleFirstIneqData_unitIdeles_le_range_of_isCyclic_of_finrank_dvd`](thm.html#NumberField.NormIndex.ideleFirstIneqData_unitIdeles_le_range_of_isCyclic_of_finrank_dvd), where a modulus admissible for the full degree has to be reused for a divisor of that degree.
